import{_ as t,o as r,c as n,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"2.3 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/2-3-the-controlled-controlled-branch-and-the-unfree-free-branch"}]],"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"2\\\\.3 受控的受控端 & 不自由的自由端 （考虑弃用）","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=t2SxyX0ruN8BvrYjwgoBde","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"2-3-the-controlled-controlled-branch-and-the-unfree-free-branch.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-t2SxyX0ruN8BvrYjwgoBde.md","lastUpdated":null}'),a={name:"2-3-the-controlled-controlled-branch-and-the-unfree-free-branch.md"};function i(s,e,l,c,d,h){return r(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="_2-3-受控的受控端-不自由的自由端-考虑弃用" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="_2-3-the-controlled-controlled-branch-and-the-unfree-free-branch-considering-retirement" tabindex="-1">2.3 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-the-controlled-controlled-branch-and-the-unfree-free-branch-considering-retirement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2\\.3 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>t2SxyX0ruN8BvrYjwgoBde</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=t2SxyX0ruN8BvrYjwgoBde" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Translation note: The historical controlled/free terminology and the source’s retirement notice are preserved.</p></blockquote><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-09ea77743101cf14.q90-6c571a76fadc76cc.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p>From the control perspective, blocking is a <strong>means of control</strong>.</p><p><span id="source-block-2RKIW4J9UCH0FvGXTyGiq9"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="分流的角度" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="splitting-perspective" tabindex="-1">Splitting Perspective <a class="header-anchor" href="#splitting-perspective" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Splitting Perspective&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Previously, when one production route divides into two and one blocks, a <strong>control relationship</strong>, or causal relationship, forms between them.</p><p>For both blocking types, the route <u><strong>we intend</strong></u> <strong>to block first is the controlled branch</strong>. <s>A route does not become controlled simply because you built it wrong and accidentally blocked it.</s> The other is the <strong>free branch</strong>. The controlled branch&#39;s rate usually faces <strong>external restrictions</strong> and changes as they change. For switching-type blocking, that restriction is <u>full inventory</u>; clearing or consuming stock restarts it. For balancing-type blocking, it is <u>a blocking flow limiter or the supply of other recipe inputs</u>; changing the limiter setting or other supplies changes production.</p><p><strong>The free branch&#39;s production is determined entirely by the controlled branch</strong>. Blocking redirects all or part of the flow toward it; clearing blocking redirects that flow back, slowing the free branch. The sequence is <strong>external restriction affects controlled production, then controlled production affects free production</strong>:</p><p><span class="mark-purple"><strong>External restriction → controlled branch → free branch</strong></span></p><p>Thus the controlled branch commonly determines upstream production from downstream blocked-buffer space/production speed. The free branch simply produces from whatever upstream gives it; without incoming material, it gets a free break.</p><p><span class="mark-blue">To put it as directly as possible: locally, the controlled branch is full storage, the free branch empty storage. Full storage can produce only as much as leaves; empty storage produces as much as enters.</span></p><blockquote><p>This matters in later gas/liquid adaptive control: the free branch cannot use output-driven control, and the controlled branch cannot use feedforward control.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>In some discussions, people also call the free branch the terminal branch.</p></blockquote><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-e0260d52f80e9397.q90-f9d858c02b714c06.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p><span class="mark-blue"><strong>In the simplest example above, the Fitting Unit branch is controlled and the Moulding Unit branch is free.</strong></span><span class="mark-blue">The controlled branch makes Ferrium Part at the Fitting Unit&#39;s maximum rate until storage fills, yet continues “listening” to the stored quantity and restarts immediately when Ferrium Part is withdrawn. The free Moulding Unit makes as many Ferrium Bottles as its Ferrium supply permits.</span></p><blockquote><p>This assumes the total input flow to free + controlled branches remains unchanged.</p></blockquote><p><strong>With priority designs,</strong> <u><strong>connect the priority output to controlled production and the secondary output to free production</strong></u>. <strong>With splitting designs, ensure</strong> <u><strong>sufficient flow to the controlled branch</strong></u> <strong>or the production goal cannot be achieved; see later.</strong></p><p>Usually, the free branch is designed to accept all flow redirected by blocking, preventing input supply from backing up. It may itself block and redirect material into a third route. It then becomes controlled relative to route three, which becomes the new free branch. The sequence becomes external restriction → route 1 → route 2 → route 3.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Controlled branch</th><th>Free branch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relative production priority</td><td>High: satisfied first</td><td>Low: does not mean no production</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>Blocked</td><td>Flowing</td></tr><tr><td>Machine buffer</td><td>Full</td><td>Empty</td></tr><tr><td>Production constraint</td><td>Blocked output buffer, missing other inputs, etc.</td><td>Missing input material</td></tr><tr><td>Flow notation (Section 2.5)</td><td>XX-</td><td>YY+</td></tr><tr><td>Causality</td><td>Rate limited by external restrictions</td><td>Rate limited to flow left over by the controlled branch</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>In Section 2.1, Ferrium Ore remaining in the depot can be viewed as a third route.</p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-tdatUvQiVv8jQqWAAVyadt"></span></p><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="汇流的角度" aria-hidden="true"></span><h3 id="merging-perspective" tabindex="-1">Merging Perspective <a class="header-anchor" href="#merging-perspective" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Merging Perspective&quot;">​</a></h3><p>As the dual case, a converger behaves oppositely. Its blocked upstream branch&#39;s flow depends on the flowing branch&#39;s flow. In the illustration, more Cuprium Ore flow means less Ferrium Ore flow.</p><p>Continue calling the blocked branch controlled and the flowing branch free. If the flowing branch faces an external restriction, control runs:</p><p><span class="mark-purple"><strong>External restriction → free branch → controlled branch</strong></span></p><p>The control direction reverses at a converger. A full belt plays the same role for a converger as an empty belt for a splitter: a converger can always take from a full belt, and a splitter can always output to an empty one.</p><p>Flow notation from Section 2.5 changes too: controlled becomes XX+, free becomes YY-.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-86409d775be21737.q90-b3990af80072a4f0.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p>Blocking-flow theory studies this thoroughly too (link to be added). Machines can behave similarly. Below, the blocked Originium Powder flow depends on Xircon flow: plainly, supplying only so much Xircon permits consuming only so much powder. Yet this control does not actually require a machine; a converger alone suffices, provided the powder blocks.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-528b1cf648896905.q90-eb50c687009a94ef.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p>The same occurs even with one material. Below, downstream accepts at most 18/m Cuprium Bottle, and the blocked Cuprium branch depends on the flowing Cuprium branch. The flowing branch&#39;s 10/m always enters the Moulding Unit. Total consumption is 36/m, leaving just 26/m for the blocked branch—a common reason an output port fails to run at full rate.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-45b149f8f087d0b8.q90-ea1c7e87c13b21c0.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p>Nevertheless, machines are not exactly convergers. In the next example, 2/3 of Ferrium Ore flow goes to the Fitting Unit and 1/3 to the Moulding Unit; the products combine in the Packaging Unit into Yazhen Syringe [C], and the Fitting Unit blocks intermittently. In the corresponding splitting/merging graph, 2/3 goes through the upper half and 1/3 the lower half before merging, yet no blocking actually occurs.</p><p>What is the difference? Put that clever little head of yours to work.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-ccde73abc1fc2989.q90-963d3167ae95758f.webp" alt="23 The Controlled Controlled Branch and the Unfree Free Branch (Considering Retirement)"></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/2-2-two-branches-bloom-switching-type-and-balancing-type-blocking">2.2 Two Branches Bloom: Switching-Type and Balancing-Type Blocking</a></p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/2-4-faster-than-light-upstream-propagation-of-blocking-signals">2.4 “Faster-Than-Light” Upstream Propagation of Blocking Signals</a></p></div>',1)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
